    "### About this Module\n",
    "This module covers **Tasks 8 and 9** of the Week (1) practical.\n",
    "Your outputs will be merged with **Modules A, B, and C into the group's final notebook**.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Background: Day 2 lecture recap:**\n",
    "\n",
    "A **distribution** describes how data values are spread across their range. \n",
    "\n",
    ">Different distributions arise from different data-generating processes:\n",
    "- **Normal:** Symmetric, bell-shaped. Defined by mean (μ) and standard deviation (σ). Common in measurement errors and biological traits.\n",
    "- **Uniform:** Every value in a range is equally likely. Common in random number generation and lottery draws.\n",
    "- **Binomial:** Counts of successes in a fixed number of independent trials, each with probability p.\n",
    "- **Poisson:** Counts of events occurring in a fixed interval at a known average rate λ.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Skewness** measures the asymmetry of a distribution. \n",
    "- Positive skew = long right tail (mean > median). \n",
    "- Negative skew = long left tail (mean < median).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Dataset:** Pima Indians Diabetes —> DOI: https://www.openml.org/d/37\n",
