    "# Task 9: Skewness: Measuring and Interpreting Asymmetry\n",
    "\n",
    "Skewness quantifies how asymmetric a distribution is. Positive skew means the right tail is longer (a few very high values pull the mean upward). Negative skew means the left tail is longer.\n",
    "\n",
    "Understanding skewness matters because:\n",
    "- It tells you whether the mean is a reliable summary (it is not, for highly skewed data)\n",
    "- Many ML models assume normally distributed inputs; skewed features may need transformation\n",
    "- It reveals domain patterns (e.g. income, insurance claims, and biological assays are often right-skewed)\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Goal:** Measure, visualise, and correct skewness in real dataset features. Understand the relationship between skewness and central tendency."
